%% CAPTION
%%
%% What this shows: the features and provenance half of the engine. Split from
%% part 1 because roughly thirty classes in one diagram is unreadable, and the
%% brief prefers two legible diagrams over one dense one.
%%
%% Three things worth a reviewer's attention. First, TerrainSource is a real
%% seam that is actually used: DemGrid consumes a TerrainLayer, so swapping the
%% synthetic DEM for real GEBCO at Checkpoint 1 changes one injected object and
%% nothing else. Second, CovariateRecipe is a Template Method precisely so that
%% provenance cannot be forgotten -- every recipe records its version, the
%% window it was asked for, the window it actually resolved to, and the border
%% policy. Third, the three provenance artifacts share a base class so that the
%% chaining fields have identical names, which is what lets a future prediction
%% be traced back to exactly one corpus and one feature stack by machine rather
%% than by convention.
%%
%% GEBCOTerrainSource is drawn with a dashed line because it does not exist
%% yet - it is what Checkpoint 1 adds.
